{"caseId":"OD2-HO-NOCONC-1","methodChoice":"EDF-2 diagnostic with two catalogue items (X12, X5)","answers":[{"itemId":"S1","answer":"unknown","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E2","X12","E3"],"}":""},{"itemId":"S2","answer":"unknown","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2","X5"]},{"itemId":"S3","answer":"unknown","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E1","E4","X12"]},{"itemId":"S4","answer":"supported","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["X12","E4","E1"]},{"itemId":"S5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E4"]},{"itemId":"S6","answer":"association-only","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E1","X12"]},{"itemId":"S7","answer":"association-only","confidence":45,"evidenceIds":["E2","X12"]},{"itemId":"S8","answer":"contradicted","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E3"]},{"itemId":"S9","answer":"preferred","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["X12","E4","E5"]},{"itemId":"S10","answer":"premature","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["X12","E2"]},{"itemId":"S11","answer":"harmful","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E3"]},{"itemId":"S12","answer":"acceptable","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["X12"]},{"itemId":"S13","answer":"harmful","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E5","E4"]},{"itemId":"S14","answer":"sufficient","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["X12","E4","E5"]}],"brief":"What is going on: the store 14 rise is probably not yet proven to be real loss. The Q1–Q3 figures are interim estimates from rotating cycle counts, and the back-test shows a typical error of about ±0.6 points per quarter. The 0.8-point rise sits right at that edge. In comparable store-years, about half of such rises were not confirmed by the year-end count. Store 14's own full-count history also swings (1.8% then 1.5%). Recorded shoplifting incidents jumped chain-wide when a new incident app launched in April, while chain shrink stayed flat, so the incident rise is partly a reporting change. The night-stocker tip was reviewed on CCTV and found unsubstantiated. Department mix did not shift. Self-checkout and footfall are plausible but unproven contributors.

How sure: moderately. The causes are not settled.

Next steps: keep store 14 in the 7 December full count and reject the proposal to drop it. Hold off on a guard, personnel action, or self-checkout changes until the full count is compared with the interim figures. Doubling cycle counts is a low-cost option. Do not suspend the named employee."}
